      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. ill grab the syslogs. \n\none thing though, i checked the interface stats on core-rt-lyon-01 and the error counters on the physical port aren't incrementing much. it's not showing layer 1 CRC errors or anything like that. but the jitter is just super inconsistent. it's not like a constant high load, it just jumps up and down randomly. could it be a routing loop or something happening in the control plane?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Indeed, inconsistent jitter can be symptomatic of transient configuration issues or signaling problems between network devices. Since you've ruled out evident layer 1 problems, the inconsistency might stem from things like changes in routing paths, ARP conflicts, or even sporadic transient issues affecting the control plane of the router.\n\nA common troubleshooting step in this case would be to check the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) logs to ensure that neighbor discovery is working properly, as well as any recent related routing updates. It's also worth verifying that no recent changes have been made to the routing protocols or firewall settings that could inadvertently create routing loops.\n\nInconsistent jitter can sometimes be observed in the presence of flapping network links or interfaces where the router frequently brings up or removes interfaces. Monitoring the status of the interface after a reboot could also be instructive. Lastly, ensure there aren't any co-located ARP caches causing collisions, especially between core and edge routers entering the ISP network.",
      "source": "target",
